  "query_preview": "/Through Monday/...\nIssued at 1149 PM MDT Mon Oct 26 2026\n\nIt's relatively quiet across the forecast area this evening. We're\njust starting to see the first bit of fog/mist develop across\nLincoln county, and stratus/mist has slowly developed along the\nnorthwest Denver metro/northern I-25 corridor over the past couple\nof hours. We still expect fog to become more widespread across the\nplains overnight tonight, with some dense fog likely over portions\nof the I-25 corridor later tonight through the AM commute Tuesday.\nSome dense fog advisories may be needed overnight, but would like\nto see the fog actually develop before trying to pinpoint where\nthe dense fog is compared to the less impactful low stratus. \n\nFog and stratus should erode pretty quickly during the morning\nhours Tuesday. Like today, temperatures will be slow to warm up\nduring the day, with highs generally in the low to mid 60s -\npotentially a bit cooler for the northern I-25 corridor. \n\nThe rest of the upcoming week looks quiet, as dry \nwest/southwesterly flow aloft continues. A return to a more active\npattern begins towards the end of this week or this weekend. A \nweak but well-defined shortwave lifts north out of New Mexico \ninto the central Great Plains on Friday. It looks like this will \ntrack just to the east of our forecast area, but there should be \nan increase in cloud cover along with a chance of showers along \nthe far eastern plains, with better chances in Kansas/Nebraska. \nAround the same time, some moisture from the Pacific reaches \nwestern Colorado either Friday or Saturday, leading to some \nscattered light rain/snow showers across the mountains to start \nthe weekend.\n\nA stronger, more organized system could approach the region by \nthe end of the weekend into next week. A strong upper trough is \nprojected to crash southeastward towards our forecast area,\npotentially interacting with the moist subtropical jet streak. As\nthe previous shift noted, there is quite a bit of spread across\nguidance. Several of deterministic models over the last 12 hours\nhave advertised the idea of a stronger upper low moving across\nColorado, though many still remain rather quiet across the state\nwith a disorganized trough quickly moving through. Ensemble\nguidance continues to show a wide distribution of solutions,\nranging from almost no precip in our CWA to some rather juicy (and\nat times, snowy) scenarios. Within the ensemble distribution,\nthere has not been a significant trend in any direction. Given\nthat we're still several days out, most of these cycle to cycle\nchanges are simply noise, and it will take some time before a\nclear trend emerges. Still, it's worth monitoring for the few\ndays in an otherwise quite forecast period.",
